Two of these deserve comment.

\code{max_iterations} is a non-static member with an in-class initialiser.
It is therefore per-object state, not a class-wide constant, and a user
can raise or lower it:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Raising the iteration budget.},label={lst:raise-budget}]
auto rules = dsl::rewrite_set (my_rule);
rules.max_iterations = 1000;
auto result = rules.apply (tree);
\end{lstlisting}

\code{apply} takes its tree \emph{by value}. This is the copy cost of
\chapref{ch:astnode} in its most visible form: the caller's tree is
copied into the parameter, and the result is copied out again. A user who
wants to rewrite a tree in place must write
\code{tree = rules.apply (std::move (tree))}, and even then the engine
copies internally, because the fixpoint loop must keep the previous
version of the tree around to compare against.

The factory \api{rewrite_set} (line 6899) is a deduction helper of the
same shape as \api{rule}:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The set factory, verbatim from the header.},label={lst:rewriteset-factory}]
template <typename... Rules>
constexpr auto
rewrite_set (Rules &&...rs)
{
  return RewriteSet<std::decay_t<Rules>...>{ std::forward<Rules> (rs)... };
}
\end{lstlisting}

Because the rules are deduced with \code{std::decay_t}, a set built from
lvalues and a set built from rvalues have the same type. Because the
order of the template arguments is the order of the arguments, the type
of the set records the rule list and its order, as
\chapref{ch:rewrite} explained.

\section{The single pass}

The heart of the engine is the private helper \api{rewrite_once} at line
6850. It is a post-order walk: the children are rewritten first, the node
is rebuilt from the rewritten children, and then the rules are tried
against the rebuilt node.

\begin{pseudocode}[One bottom-up pass]
\textbf{function} rewrite\_once (n)\\
\quad changed \code{<-} false\\
\quad new\_children \code{<-} empty vector\\
\quad \textbf{if} n is not a leaf \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{for} c \textbf{in} n.children \textbf{do}\\
\qquad\quad (did, rewritten) \code{<-} rewrite\_once (c)\\
\qquad\quad changed \code{<-} changed \textbf{or} did\\
\qquad\quad append rewritten to new\_children\\
\quad \textbf{if} n is a leaf \textbf{then}\\
\qquad current \code{<-} n\\
\quad \textbf{else}\\
\qquad current \code{<-} ASTNode (n.tag, move (new\_children))\\
\quad \textbf{for} r \textbf{in} rules \textbf{do}\\
\qquad \textbf{if} r.try\_apply (current) \emph{has a value} \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\quad current \code{<-} that value\\
\qquad\quad changed \code{<-} true\\
\quad \textbf{return} (changed, current)
\end{pseudocode}

Four properties of this pass are worth stating one at a time.

\paragraph{The pass is bottom-up.}
The children are rewritten before the node itself is examined. This has
an important consequence: a rule that matches a parent whose children have
just been rewritten sees the \emph{rewritten} children. A rule that
recognises \code{add(num 1, num 2)} fires in the same pass in which the
two numbers were folded --- provided the folding rule comes first in the
rule list, or provided the fold happens at a deeper level. Bottom-up
ordering is what makes a single pass able to accomplish a great deal.

\paragraph{The rebuilt node drops the value of an inner node.}
The reconstruction is written as

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The reconstruction performed by \api{rewrite_once}.},label={lst:rebuild}]
ASTNode current
    = n.is_leaf () ? n : ASTNode{ n.tag (), std::move (new_children) };
\end{lstlisting}

For a leaf the whole node is copied, value included. For an inner node
the tag and the new children are used, and the inner-node constructor
leaves the value empty. In practice this is unobservable, because an inner
node cannot have a non-empty value through the public API in the first
place --- but it is worth knowing that the rebuild is not a
field-by-field copy.

\paragraph{Every matching rule fires, in order.}
The rule loop is a fold over the tuple, and the fold evaluates all of its
elements. There is no \code{break} and no short-circuit: if three rules
match the current node, all three fire, and the second and third see the
node produced by the first. This is the mechanism behind the \code{chain}
line of Listing~\ref{lst:rewriteset-demo}: the rule that turns \code{"a"}
into \code{"b"} fires, and then the rule that turns \code{"b"} into
\code{"c"} fires on the result, so the final answer is \code{"(c 0)"} in a
single pass.

\paragraph{The pass reports whether anything changed.}
The boolean in the returned pair is set when a rule fires --- and it is set
when a rule fires, \emph{not} when a rule produces a different node. A
rule whose transformer returns the node it was given still counts as a
change. This is the conservative choice, and it is what makes the
fixpoint loop safe in the presence of a rule that is idempotent but not
identity-preserving.

\section{The fixpoint loop}

\begin{pseudocode}[The outer fixpoint loop]
\textbf{function} apply (root)\\
\quad \textbf{for} i \code{<-} 0 \textbf{to} max\_iterations - 1 \textbf{do}\\
\qquad (changed, result) \code{<-} rewrite\_once (root)\\
\qquad \textbf{if} changed is false \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\quad \textbf{return} result\\
\qquad root \code{<-} move (result)\\
\quad \textbf{return} root
\end{pseudocode}

The loop runs until a \emph{whole pass} reports no change. That is a
stronger statement than "no rule fired on the root": a pass that changes
a leaf deep in the tree reports a change even though the root is
untouched, and the loop continues.

\figref{fig:fixpoint-loop} draws the loop.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\begin{dslgraph}
  \node[dslbox] (start) {\code{apply(root)}\\the tree, by value};
  \node[dslbox, text width=44mm, below=8mm of start] (pass)
    {\code{rewrite_once(root)}\\one bottom-up pass};
  \node[dsldecision, below=8mm of pass, text width=26mm] (test)
    {did the pass report\\a change?};

  \node[dslbox, below=10mm of test, xshift=-34mm] (done)
    {\code{return result}\\the fixpoint};
  \node[dslbox, text width=44mm, below=10mm of test, xshift=34mm] (move)
    {\code{root = std::move(result)}\\next iteration};
  \node[dslbox, below=8mm of move] (budget)
    {another iteration\\available?};

  \draw[dsledge] (start) -- (pass);
  \draw[dsledge] (pass) -- (test);
  \draw[dsledge] (test) -- node[above] {no} (done);
  \draw[dsledge] (test) -- node[above] {yes} (move);
  \draw[dsledge] (move) -- (budget);
  \draw[dslfeedback] (budget.east) to[out=0, in=0] node[right] {yes} (pass.east);
  \draw[dsledge] (budget.south) |- node[below] {no} (done.south);
\end{dslgraph}
\caption{The fixpoint loop. A pass that reports no change ends the loop; a
pass that reports a change is repeated until the budget is exhausted, at
