\label{fig:fixpoint-loop}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The pass and the loop are different things}

The distinction between the inner pass and the outer loop is easy to
blunder, so it is worth making concrete.

The inner pass is bottom-up and fires every rule that matches. It
therefore handles most transformations in one go: a constant folded deep
in the tree is visible to its parent in the same pass, and a parent whose
child was just rewritten is tested against the rewritten child.

The outer loop exists for the cases the inner pass cannot reach. A rule
that produces a node matching an \emph{earlier} rule in the list is the
canonical case. In Listing~\ref{lst:rewriteset-demo} the \code{late}
demonstration is exactly that: the rule set contains \code{one-to-done}
followed by \code{two-to-one}. A tree \code{(two (three))} is rewritten in
the first pass to \code{(one (three))}, because \code{two-to-one} fires
and \code{one-to-done} did not match --- it ran first, on a node tagged
\code{"two"}. Only in the second pass does \code{one-to-done} match, and
the third pass confirms that nothing more changes. The output records
three passes, and that count is exactly right: the pass that reports no
change is counted too.

The same reasoning applies to rules whose output creates a redex at a
\emph{higher} level of the tree. Bottom-up ordering means the parent is
tested after the child in the same pass, so a rule that creates a parent
redex is normally caught immediately. But a rule whose output moves a
redex \emph{upward} --- a rule that wraps its result in a new node, say ---
can expose a redex that did not exist before, and the outer loop is what
catches it.

\subsection{Copies}

Every pass copies the tree. The reconstruction of the node copies the
rewritten children into a fresh vector, and the vector's elements are
copied into the new node. The cost of one pass over an \(N\)-node tree is
therefore \(O(N)\) node copies in the best case and \(O(N \cdot d)\), where
\(d\) is the average depth, in the worst case, because each node's
children are copied once per ancestor that is rebuilt.

The cost of the whole fixpoint computation is that per pass, multiplied by
the number of passes. A rule set that converges in three passes over a
thousand-node tree performs on the order of three thousand node
allocations --- which is the dominant cost of a rewriting phase, and the
reason the in-place traversal of \chapref{ch:traversal} is worth
considering for hot paths.

\section{The silent truncation}

The loop has a budget, and exceeding it is not an error. When
\code{max_iterations} is exhausted, \api{apply} returns the tree produced
by the last pass --- no exception, no diagnostic, no flag, no return code.
The \code{trunc} line of Listing~\ref{lst:rewriteset-demo} shows this: a
rule that increments a numeric leaf by one can never reach a fixpoint, so
the loop runs its four iterations and returns a tree whose leaf is
\code{4} instead of stopping.

\begin{warnbox}[Non-convergence is invisible]
Because there is no diagnostic, a user who writes a non-terminating rule
set sees a plausible-looking result and no indication that the budget was
exhausted. The only reliable defences are to design the rules so that
each one strictly decreases a well-founded measure, and to check the
result against what the rules were supposed to achieve. If the budget is
the binding constraint, raise it:
\code{rules.max_iterations = 1000;} works, because the member is public
and non-static.
\end{warnbox}

Two failure modes deserve separate mention.

The first is a rule whose transformer returns its input unchanged. Such a
rule fires, the pass reports a change, and the fixpoint loop runs to
exhaustion --- producing exactly the same tree at every iteration, and
returning it without complaint. The engine cannot distinguish "changed
but identically" from "changed", because the change flag records that a
rule fired, not that the result differs.

The second is a pair of rules that undo each other: \code{a} becomes
\code{b} and \code{b} becomes \code{a}. This does not loop forever within
a pass, because the fold stops after the rules have each been tried once
--- but it does change the tree on every pass, so the outer loop never
reaches a fixpoint and the budget is exhausted. The two rules are tried in
the order they appear in the list, and the last one to fire wins.

\section{No merge, no concatenation}

There is no \code{operator+} on \api{RewriteSet}, no \code{merge}, no
\code{concat}, and no way to combine two sets. The rule list is fixed at
construction, in the type and in the tuple.

A user who wants the union of two sets must write one set with all the
rules:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Combining rule sets by writing one set.},label={lst:combine-sets}]
auto combined = dsl::rewrite_set (rule_a, rule_b, rule_c, rule_d);
// there is no combined = set_one + set_two;
\end{lstlisting}

This is a consequence of the type-level encoding rather than an
oversight: since the set's type records its rules, two sets can only be
combined if the result is a third set, and the toolkit does not define
what the order of the combined rules should be. The eager answer --- write
the union out --- is also the answer that preserves the property that
matters most: the order of the rules is the order of the arguments, and
that order is visible in the source.

\section{Bottom-up, top-down, and saturation}

Three strategies for transforming a tree are worth contrasting, because
\DSLtk{} implements the first and offers the third.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{2.9cm}L{3.85cm}L{3.85cm}L{3.85cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Aspect}
  & \textbf{Bottom-up (\api{RewriteSet})}
  & \textbf{Top-down (user-written)}
  & \textbf{Saturation (\api{EGraph})} \\
\midrule
Order
  & children first
  & parent first
  & ordered passes retaining alternatives \\
Search space
  & one tree
  & one tree
  & a set of equivalent terms \\
Output
  & one resulting tree
  & one resulting tree
  & a minimum-node representative \\
Rules
  & predicate and transformer
  & predicate and transformer
  & \code{(rewrite name lhs rhs)} with \code{?variables} \\
Termination
  & bounded by \code{max_iterations}
  & by the user's discipline
  & by \code{SaturationLimits} \\
Reporting
  & none; silent truncation
  & none
  & \code{SaturationResult} with a stop reason \\
Cost
  & \(O(\text{passes} \times N)\) copies
  & \(O(\text{passes} \times N)\) copies
  & grows with the number of e-nodes \\
Best at
  & normalisation and simplification
  & goal-directed, local rewrites
  & optimisation under competing rewrites \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Three transformation strategies. DSLtk supplies bottom-up rewriting
and equality saturation; top-down traversal can be implemented by the user.}
\label{tab:rewrite-strategies}
\end{table}

A top-down rewriter is one the user writes: it tests a node, and if the
node matches, it transforms it and does not descend into the children;
otherwise it descends. The advantage is precision --- the rewriter stops
as soon as it has what it wanted --- and the disadvantage is that a
transformation which creates a new opportunity higher up the tree is
missed, unless the user writes the recursion to loop.

Equality saturation, in \chapref{ch:egraphs}, takes a third route
entirely. Instead of transforming one tree, it maintains a set of terms
that are all equal to the original, adding a new term for every match of
every rule and never deleting any. The search is exhaustive by
construction, and the final answer is extracted by choosing the cheapest
representative. The price is that the set of terms grows, and that the
extraction step needs a cost function.

The distinction to keep in mind is that the rewrite set is a
\emph{normaliser}: it searches for a single tree in which no rule applies.
Saturation is an \emph{optimiser}: it searches for the best tree among
all the ones the rules can reach. They answer different questions, and
\chapref{ch:case-saturation} builds a case study that uses both.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\paragraph{The budget is silent.}
There is no error, no exception, no flag, and no way to ask a set whether
its last run converged. \api{RewriteSet} has no state beyond
\code{max_iterations}, so it cannot remember that it ran out of budget.

\paragraph{The budget counts passes, not depth.}
\code{max_iterations} bounds the number of passes over the tree. It does
nothing about the depth of a single pass, so a deep tree can still
overflow the stack, as \chapref{ch:traversal} explained.

\paragraph{The change flag is conservative.}
A rule that fires counts as a change even if it produced an identical
node. A rule set that is idempotent but not identity-preserving will
therefore exhaust the budget rather than converge.

\paragraph{Rules are tried in list order, and all of them.}
There is no priority, no specificity ordering, and no first-match-wins
semantics. A later rule sees the output of an earlier one. Two rules that
both match and both transform produce a result determined purely by
their order in the argument list.

\paragraph{The tree is copied in and copied out.}
\api{apply} takes its argument by value and returns by value. A caller
who rewrites a large tree pays for at least two full copies of it.

\paragraph{One pass at a time is not available.}
\api{rewrite_once} is private. A user who wants to observe the
intermediate trees --- to print them, to count them, to impose their own
budget --- cannot, and must either reimplement the pass or infer the
behaviour from the final result.

\paragraph{No diagnostics.}
There is no rule-tracing facility, no log of which rules fired, and no
counter of how many times each rule fired. The counting rule of
Listing~\ref{lst:rewriteset-demo} is a user-written substitute, and it
works only because a rule can capture a reference and inspect the node it
is given.

\paragraph{No parallelism.}
The pass is a sequential recursion over a tuple of rules. The rules
themselves may be called concurrently if the user arranges it, but the
engine offers nothing.

\paragraph{An empty rule set is legal.}
\code{dsl::rewrite_set()} produces a set with no rules. Its
\code{apply} performs one pass, reports no change, and returns the tree
unchanged --- a copy of the input, in fact.

\section{Closing}

\api{RewriteSet} is the whole rewriting machinery of \DSLtk{}: a tuple of
rules, a budget, and a loop that repeats a bottom-up pass until a pass
reports no change. Its design decisions are all visible in its behaviour:
children are rewritten before their parents, every matching rule fires,
the rules are tried in the order they were written, and the loop stops
when nothing changes --- or when the budget runs out, which it does
without saying so. The alternative strategies are set up in
\tabref{tab:rewrite-strategies} and developed in \chapref{ch:egraphs} and
\chapref{ch:case-saturation}; the next chapter leaves the AST subsystem
entirely and turns to the expression templates of
\chapref{ch:expression-templates}.
